  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 258 PM EDT SUN JUL 26 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Tuesday morning with a pronounced ~596\ndam high over the Southern Rockies with a ridge axis extending \nnorthward into Central Canada. Meanwhile, troughing extends \nsouthward from an around 540 dam low over northern Quebec to along\nthe Mid-Atlantic Coast. An embedded shortwave trough is present \nover the Lower Great Lakes. This will be mirrored at the surface \nby a 997 mb surface low over Lake Erie. A warm front extends \nsouthward from that low to over the Southern Appalachians while a \ncold front arcs southwest from the surface low over southern \nIndiana and Illinois and then beyond to over the High Plains of \nMontana. This will leave eastern Kentucky in the low's warm \nsector at daybreak Tuesday morning with a seasonably moist air \nmass (PWATs around 1.8 inches).\n\nThe surface low will shift eastward along the PA/NY Twin Tiers \nthrough the day and evening on Tuesday, dropping the cold front \nacross the Commonwealth. Substantial uncertainty remains as to \ninstability recovery ahead of this boundary given the likelihood for \nconvection early in the day, and especially prior to sunrise with a \npotential MCS. If the frontal arrival is slower and greater \ndestabilization occurs, unseasonably strong northwesterly flow aloft \nwould support robust shear and organized convection. Conversely, \na quicker frontal passage would tend to favor keeping any stronger\norganized convection confined to far southeastern Kentucky or \nperhaps entirely southeast of our CWA. In addition to the \nconvective potential, Tuesday does appear breezy with some \nmomentum transfer mixing to the surface from a low-level jet (30+ \nkt at 850 hPa). The high PWATs could also favor some isolated \nheavy rainfall. Any hydro problems would probably occur where \nsubstantial rainfall occurred during the last 12 hours of the \nshort-term period; by Tuesday, the strong flow aloft should keep \ncell motions quite progressive.\n\nOnce the front clears the area, cooler and drier air will filter in \non Tuesday night and Wednesday, with LREF mean precipitable water \nvalues dropping to between 0.75 and 1.00 inch, or near the 10th \npercentile relative to climatology. Aloft, ridging will remain over \nthe West while troughing lingers over Eastern Canada and much of \nthe Eastern CONUS. Surface high pressure drifting south from \nOntario and Quebec will ensure dry conditions through Friday. \nAnother shortwave trough drops into the broader trough during the\nupcoming weekend. This will foster another surface low developing\nover the Plains and drifting toward the Tennessee Valley \nvicinity, bringing back higher PWATs and the renewed threat of \nconvection for Saturday and Sunday.\n\nIn sensible weather terms, look for a high chance of showers and \nthunderstorms (PoPs of 60 to 90 percent, highest in far eastern \nand southeastern Kentucky) on Tuesday. If substantial sunshine \nmaterializes, some of the storms could be strong to severe and \nalso produce heavy downpours. It will be warm and humid with highs\nin the 80s. Fair weather follows for Tuesday night through Friday\nwith highs in the low to mid 80s and lows mostly from 60 to 65F. \nAs the threat of showers and thunderstorms returns for Saturday \nand Sunday, daily high temperatures will remain at similar levels,\nbut nighttime lows will moderate to between 65 and 70F with \nhigher humidity levels.",
